L1: Cornu cervi pantotrichum  supplementation improves physiological adaptions during intensive endurance training
L2: The Journal of Veterinary Medical Science (2017)
L3: Wen-Ching HUANG, Chi-Chang HUANG, Hsiao-Li CHUANG, Chien-Chao CHIU, Wen-Chyuan CHEN, Mei-Chich HSU
L4: 1) Department of Exercise and Health Science, National Taipei University of Nursing and Health Sciences, Taipei 11219, Taiwan
L5: 2) National Taiwan Sport University, Taoyuan 33301, Taiwan
L6: 3) National Laboratory Animal Center, National Applied Research Laboratories, Taipei 11529, Taiwan
L7: 4) Center for General Education, Chang Gung University of Science and Technology, Taoyuan 33301, Taiwan
L8: 5) Department of Otorhinolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Sleep Center, Linkou-Chang Gung Memorial Hospital, Taoyuan 33301,
L9: Taiwan
L10: 6) Department of Sports Medicine, Kaohsiung Medical University, Kaohsiung 80708, Taiwan
L11: 7) Kaohsiung Medical University Hospital, Kaohsiung 80708, Taiwan
L12: Cornu cervi pantotrichum  (CCP), used in traditional Chinese medicine, is a well-known yang-invigorating agent with multifunctional
L13: bioactivities. We previously showed, through an acute exercise challenge, that short-term CCP supplementation improved physical activities and
L14: fatigue-associated biochemical indices. Questions about the long-term effects of CCP treatment on exercise performance and physical fatigue, as well as safety,
L15: with intensive exercise training need further research. ICR-strain mice were randomly assigned to three groups: (1) sedentary control and vehicle treatment
L16: (SC); (2) exercise training with vehicle treatment (ET); and (3) ET with CCP treatment at 4,108 mg/kg/day (ET+CCP). We assessed the physical performance, body
L17: compositions, and serum levels of lactate, ammonia, glucose and creatine kinase (CK) after an acute exercise challenge. The ET and ET+CCP groups had
L18: significantly increased grip strength and endurance swimming time, and decreased serum lactate and ammonia levels after the acute exercise challenge than the SC
L19: group. Moreover, serum ammonia and CK levels in the ET+CCP group were significantly decreased when compared to that of the ET only group. In regard to the body
L20: composition, the ET+CCP group inhibits the decrease in fat tissue, and related biochemical changes induced by the high intensity endurance training CCP
L21: supplementation combined with high-intensity endurance exercise could significantly improve the physiological adaptions related to fatigue or energy consumption
L22: and maintain the fat composition when compared to treatment with training only. Therefore, CCP may potentially improve the physiological adaptions in intensive
L23: exercise training.
L24: Physiology; Full Paper; Full Paper
L25: ammonia; Chinese yang agents; creatine kinase; intensive training; physiological adaption
L26: MATERIALS AND METHODS
L27: Materials
L28: The CCP used in this study was prepared by ethanol extraction (International Total Solution, Inc., Taipei City, Taiwan) with quality and safety certification.
L29: Briefly, the raw CCP materials were sliced and extracted three times with a material-ethanol ratio of 1:5. The extracts were filtered to remove debris and
L30: concentrated by vacuum drying. The final product obtained was the CCP powder, which was used in this study.
L31: Animals and experiment design
L32: Male 6-week-old ICR-strain mice, purchased from BioLASCO (Yi-Lan, Taiwan), with a specific pathogen-free condition were used in this study. All animals were
L33: given a standard laboratory diet (No. 5001; PMI Nutrition International, Brentwood, MO, U.S.A.), distilled water  ad libitum , and maintained
L34: under a regular photoperiod (12-hr light/12-hr dark) at the conditioned temperature and humidity (24 ± 1°C; 60 ± 5%). The Institutional Animal Care and Use
L35: Committee (IACUC) of National Taiwan Sport University evaluated all animal experiments and approved this study as protocol number IACUC-10206.
L36: The administrated doses for this animal experiment could be converted from a human-equivalent dose (HED) based on body surface area, provided by the U.S. Food
L37: and Drug Administration, by using different conversion coefficients according to different animal species; the conversion coefficient is 12.3 for mouse species.
L38: The administrated dose of CCP in the current study was 4,018 mg/kg of body weight (BW) for the animal experiments, which was equivalent to the recommended dose
L39: of approximately 20 g for a 60-kg human. The details of the formula are described in a previous study [ 5 ].
L40: All experimental mice were provided with a 1-week acclimation period to adjust to their new environment and diet. Subsequently, they were randomly assigned to
L41: three groups (8 mice/group) for the training treatment with either the vehicle or CCP. The groups were as follows: (1) the sedentary control treatment (SC), (2)
L42: intensive aerobic exercise training (ET) or (3) the intensive aerobic exercise training with 4,108 mg/kg of CCP supplementation (ET+CCP). The sedentary and ET
L43: groups received the same volume of vehicle solution dose-equivalent for their individual body weight. The vehicle and CCP were administrated to each animal by
L44: oral gavage daily for consecutive 6 weeks.
L45: ET protocol
L46: Fig. 1. Protocol for the 6-week swimming exercise training.
L47: In previous definitions of animal swimming endurance, the loading, less than 3% equivalent to the body weight, was defined as aerobic exercise. A frequency of
L48: three weekly sections was considered a moderate training protocol, and a five times a week was considered heavy training [ 24 ]. Animals in the ET and ET+CCP groups underwent an aerobic swimming training program adapted from our previous study [ 5 ]. The specifications of the container were 65 cm and 20 cm in high and diameter, respectively, filled with tap water to a depth of 40 cm,
L49: and maintained at 28 ± 1°C. They were trained for 30 min on the first day, 45 min on the second day and 60 min a day thereafter, for 5 days/week during the
L50: first week to acclimatize them to water. After the mice had adapted to the swimming exercise, they were subjected to a forced swimming task with a weight,
L51: equivalent to 1% to 2% of their body weight, attached to their tails for 5 weeks. The training period consisted of 5 weeks, 60 min per day and 5 sessions per
L52: week. The mice began the forced swimming training with a 1% body weight overload attached to their bodies in the first week, i.e., week 2. The training load was
L53: then increased to 2% of their body weight during the last 4 weeks (i.e., weeks 3−6). The training protocol is illustrated in  Fig. 1 Fig. 1. Protocol for the 6-week swimming exercise training. . Body weight was measured weekly, and the load was calculated and increased accordingly.
L54: Forelimb grip strength
L55: The forelimb grip strength of mice undergoing vehicle or CCP treatments was measured by a low-force testing system (Model-RX-5, Aikoh Engineering, Nagoya,
L56: Japan). The amount of tensile force exerted by each mouse was measured using a force transducer equipped with a metal bar (2 mm in diameter and 7.5 cm in
L57: length). The detailed procedures are described in our previous reports [ 10 ]. The grip strength was recorded as the
L58: maximal force (g).
L59: Swimming exercise performance test
L60: The swim-to-exhaustion exercise test involved mice carrying constant loads corresponding to 5% BW and assessed endurance time, as previously described [ 5 ,  13 ,  14 ].
L61: Determination of fatigue-associated serum biomarkers
L62: The effects of ET and ET+CCP intervention on serum lactate, ammonia and glucose levels and creatine kinase (CK) activity were evaluated after exercise. The
L63: same intensity of challenge, a 15-min swimming test without weight-loading, was performed after 6 weeks from administration. Blood samples were immediately
L64: collected from the submandibular duct of the animals after the swimming exercise. The serum was prepared by centrifugation at 1,500 × g, 4°C for 10 min.
L65: Lactate, ammonia and glucose levels and CK activity were determined by using an auto-analyzer (Hitachi 7060, Hitachi, Tokyo, Japan). Other clinical biochemical
L66: variables were determined using an automatic analyzer (Hitachi 7080, Hitachi) at the end of experiment without fasting.
L67: Histological staining of tissues
L68: After sacrificing the mice, the important organs including the liver, muscles (gastrocnemius and soleus muscles of the lower legs), kidney, testes, epididymal
L69: fat pad (EPF), brown adipocyte tissue (BAT), heart and lung were dissected, weighed and snap-frozen in liquid nitrogen before they were stored at –80°C. Another
L70: set of liver, muscle, heart, kidney, lung and testes tissues was removed intact and fixed in 10% neutral-buffered formalin for at least 24 hr for
L71: histopathologic evaluation, as previously described [ 5 ]. Tissues were embedded in paraffin, cut into 4- μ m
L72: thick slices for morphological and pathological evaluation, stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E), and examined using a light microscope equipped with a
L73: charge-coupled device (CCD) camera (BX-51, Olympus, Tokyo, Japan).
L74: Statistical analysis
L75: Data are expressed as mean ± SEM. Statistical differences among groups were analyzed by a one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) using SAS, v9.0 (SAS Inst.,
L76: Cary, NC, U.S.A.). Tukey (HSD) post-hoc analysis was used for further comparisons within each testing group. A  P  value <0.05 was considered
L77: statistically significant.
L78: RESULTS
