L92: Effect of CCP supplementation with training on fatigue-associated biomarkers after exercise challenge
L93: Fig. 4. Effect of  Cornu cervi pantotrichum  supplementation with training on serum (a) lactate; (b) ammonia; (c) glucose; and (d) creatine kinase
L94: (CK) levels after an acute exercise challenge. Data are expressed as mean ± SEM for the 8 mice in each group. Different letters indicate a significant
L95: difference of  P <0.05, assessed by a one-way ANOVA.
L96: The status of muscle fatigue after exercise can be accessed by important biochemical biomarkers, including lactate, ammonia and glucose levels and CK activity
L97: [ 5 ,  10 ,  13 ,  14 ,  16 ,  21 ]. As shown in  Fig. 4a Fig. 4. Effect of  Cornu cervi pantotrichum  supplementation with training on serum (a) lactate; (b) ammonia; (c) glucose; and (d) creatine kinase
L98: (CK) levels after an acute exercise challenge. Data are expressed as mean ± SEM for the 8 mice in each group. Different letters indicate a significant
L99: difference of  P <0.05, assessed by a one-way ANOVA. , serum lactate levels significantly differed among the treatment groups (F[2,21]=92.38,  P <0.05, η2=0.898). It was reduced by 52% in
L100: ET mice and by 59.6% (all  P <0.001) in ET+CCP mice, respectively, when compared to the SC group. The serum lactate level in ET+CCP group was
L101: not significantly lower than the ET only group ( P =0.137). Serum ammonia levels differed significantly among the treatment groups
L102: (F[2,21]=25.21,  P <0.05, η2=0.706). ET and ET+CCP treatments significantly decreased serum ammonia levels by 34.7% and 70%
L103: ( P =0.0236 and  P <0.0001), respectively, relative to SC ( Fig. 4b ). Remarkably,
L104: serum ammonia levels in the ET+CCP group were also significantly lower than those in the ET only group ( P =0.0210).
L105: During exercise, efficient utilization of glucose is an important index for maintaining performance by a mechanism independent of the insulin signaling pathway
L106: [ 20 ]. However, the glucose levels were not significantly different between the groups. The CK levels significantly
L107: differed between the treatment groups (F[2,21]= 4.39,  P <0.05, η2=0.295) ( Fig. 4c ). Although ET
L108: treatment led to a reduction in CK levels, compared to SC, this reduction was not statistically significant ( Fig. 4d ).
L109: ET+CCP treatment reduced CK levels by 67.5% ( P =0.0110), in comparison to SC.
L110: Effect of CCP supplementation with training on the general characteristics of the growth curve, diet and organ weights
